    "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-td-accessibility-a1-a6",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the following axioms for $\\prec$ on every declared pair of finite compounds with equal formal mass in [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]]. Initially this covers equal-composition adiabatic accessibility. If a later channel model admits comparisons between distinct composition types, the same axioms apply to those comparisons and all their finite scaled products; no cross-type transition is admitted by this postulate alone. They are assumptions of the operational model, not consequences of the process definition or of energy additivity.\n\n**A1 (reflexivity).** $A\\prec A$ for every state $A$.\n\n**A2 (transitivity).** If $A\\prec B$ and $B\\prec C$, then $A\\prec C$.\n\n**A3 (consistency).** If $A\\prec B$, then $A+C\\prec B+C$ for every finite spectator compound $C$ for which both endpoint compounds are admitted and the resulting formal masses agree.\n\n**A4 (scaling).** If $A\\prec B$, then $\\lambda A\\prec\\lambda B$ for every $\\lambda>0$, using the tagged scaling maps of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]].\n\n**A5 (splitting).** For each state $x$, positive $s<t$, and tagged scaled copies of its material, the state $t x$ is accessible in both directions to the separated pair $(s x,(t-s)x)$. This states reversible splitting of an equilibrium preparation into scaled copies, not additivity of arbitrary state functions.\n\n**A6 (stability).** Let $A,B$ have equal formal mass. Let $Z_0,Z_1$ be fixed positive spectator compounds of equal formal mass, and let $\\epsilon_n>0$ decrease to zero. If $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\prec B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for every $n$, then $A\\prec B$.\n\nEquality of formal masses in A6 is required so each comparison is defined; equality of composition vectors is not required when a declared model includes cross-type transitions. A6 is a limiting closure assumption on the relation; it does not assert continuity of any physical process or the existence of a limiting apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For each simple-system chart $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$, $n_i\\ge1$, let $x=(u,v)$, with one energy coordinate $u$ and $n_i$ work coordinates $v$. The chart is a nonempty open convex domain. The simple-compressible physical case is the fixed-composition slice and embedding of [[def-td-thermal-join]], with $n_i=1$, $u=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $v=V/(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$. A declared multi-work chart or joined chart instead supplies its own equilibrium endpoint domain and diagonal unit map: $U=u(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $W_a=v_a w_a$ for fixed positive work-coordinate units $w_a$. The numerical domain is identified with these physical endpoints by that supplied map, and accessibility is pulled back from the admitted endpoint relation. These identifications preserve the internal-energy meaning and fixed composition; extra work coordinates do not follow from the simple-compressible state definition. Adopt these assumptions for every state $x$ and for every thermal join treated as a simple system:\n\n**A7 (convex-combination accessibility).** For $0<t<1$, the separated scaled pair $(t x,(1-t)y)$ is accessible to the chart state $t x+(1-t)y$, whenever these states are in the declared domains.\n\nFor each $x$, define its forward sector $A_x=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:x\\prec y\\}$. Adopt:\n\n**S1 (strict successor).** Some $y\\in\\Gamma_i$ satisfies $x\\prec\\prec y$.\n\n**S2 (unique finite-slope support).** At $x=(u_x,v_x)$, $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane through $x$, and it can be written\n$$\nu-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0\n$$\nwith finite $P_i(x)\\in\\mathbb R^{n_i}$. The map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$ is locally Lipschitz: every point has a neighborhood on which one finite Lipschitz constant bounds $\\|P_i(x)-P_i(y)\\|/\\|x-y\\|$. A supporting hyperplane means $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets it at $x$.\n\n**S3 (connected relative boundary).** The boundary $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$, taken in the topology relative to $\\Gamma_i$, is arcwise connected.\n\nThese are explicit adopted conditions. The slope $P_i$ is a geometric support parameter here; no equation identifying it with measured pressure is asserted. In particular, S1–S3 and A7 do not assert comparison hypothesis for products.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A3",
          "source": "post-td-thermal-join-axioms",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Use thermal joins and $\\sim_T$ exactly as defined in [[def-td-thermal-join]]. For every ordered pair of simple systems and every admitted finite join, adopt:\n\n**T1 (joining).** $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$.\n\n**T2 (reversible splitting).** Every join state has at least one split into component states at its stated total energy and work coordinates that is reversible to the join state. Also, for every state $x$ and every $0<t<1$, the equal-state scaled split $(t x,(1-t)x)$ is reversible to $x$.\n\n**T3 (thermal-equilibrium transitivity).** If $x\\sim_T y$ and $y\\sim_T z$ in the model’s declared contact family, then $x\\sim_T z$. This requires the model to provide the relevant joined types and identifications.\n\n**T4 (transversality).** For each simple-system state $x$, there are $x_0,x_1$ in that system with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$.\n\nThe axioms apply to admitted types and their joins only. T4 is an assumption about the contact relation; it is not a consequence of an equation of state or of a thermometer reading. The stronger range condition T5 is stated separately in [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and join coherence C1 is separately stated in [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A3",
          "source": "def-td-thermal-join",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the simple-compressible physical branch, a primitive label is a pair $i=(c_i,n^i)$ of constitution and fixed positive mole amounts. Its numerical state domain is exactly $\\Gamma_i=\\Gamma_{c_i,n^i}$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], with embedding $b_i(u,v)=(u,v,\\hat n^i)\\in D_{c_i}$. The unit map sends $(u,v)$ to $(u\\,\\mathrm J,v\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$; thus $u$ is the numerical value of the fixed internal energy, not a newly oriented energy coordinate. The underlying accessibility is the pullback of the admitted physical endpoint relation along $b_i$. Positive bulk scaling uses the declared scaling of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], carries the mole vector to $\\lambda n^i$, and retains the distinct scaled tag. More general work coordinates or formal thermometer examples require their own explicitly declared domain, coordinate unit map and endpoint relation; no identification with $D_c$ is supplied by this definition.\n\nFor two named simple-system types $i,j$ with energy/work coordinates $x=(u_i,v_i)$ and $y=(u_j,v_j)$, a thermal join is the model-declared compound state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ whose energy coordinate is $u_i+u_j$ and whose work coordinates retain the ordered pair $(v_i,v_j)$. Its join domain consists exactly of those tuples $(u,v_i,v_j)$ for which there are $u_i,u_j$ with $(u_i,v_i)$ and $(u_j,v_j)$ in the component domains and $u=u_i+u_j$. This is a tagged join state, distinct from the separated pair $(x,y)$, even when their total coordinates agree. The joined domain retains both work coordinates and has aggregate mole composition $n^i+n^j$ (in a common declared species list); it is not thereby a simple-compressible slice of one $D_c$. The contact model supplies its joined equilibrium endpoint space, endpoint coordinate map and admitted process relation. The contact model declares the pair encoding and join encoding to have the same aggregate composition and extends its accessibility relation to these two encodings; the contact axioms specify which such comparisons are admitted.\n\nWrite $x\\sim_T y$ exactly when the pair $(x,y)$ and its join state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ are accessible in both directions under the declared contact operation. Here $\\sim_T$ is a relation between states of the two named systems; it is not numerical equality of temperature. Nested joins use only the model’s declared reassociation and permutation identifications. The coordinate sum alone supplies no accessibility comparison.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A4",
          "source": "post-td-universal-thermal-range",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt **T5 (universal thermal-equilibrium range)**: for every ordered pair of simple systems $i,j$, each state $x$ of $i$, and every work-coordinate point $v_j$ in the projection of the state domain of $j$, there is some state $y=(u_j,v_j)$ of $j$ with $x\\sim_T y$, using [[def-td-thermal-join]].\n\nThis is a quantified existence assumption for a thermal-equilibrium partner at each permitted work coordinate. It does not define a numerical temperature, assert that all systems possess the same temperature range as a real apparatus, or supply a calibration map and its uncertainty.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A5",
          "source": "post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt **C1 (equilibrium-join coherence)**: whenever $x\\sim_T y$, the joined state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ is thermally equivalent to each constituent, $J_{ij}(x,y)\\sim_T x$ and $J_{ij}(x,y)\\sim_T y$, using the model’s declared nested-join identifications.\n\nC1 is an additional postulate of this completed contact branch. The source’s Theorem 4.8 is stated with T1–T4; the local conditional calibrator route uses the constituent-to-join bridge in addition to the stronger T5 range assumption. Neither fact licenses deriving C1 from $u=u_i+u_j$, from T1–T4, or from transitivity alone. The displayed source supports the contact context, not the claim that its bare printed axioms already contain C1.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma_2$ be simple domains in a family of finite thermal joins satisfying A1–A7, S1–S3, T1–T5, and C1 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]]. Then there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $\\Gamma_1$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $\\Gamma_2$ such that\n\n$$(x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0).$$\n\nMoreover, for every fixed finite composition of scaled copies of $\\Gamma_1$ and $\\Gamma_2$, any two equal-mass states of that same composition are comparable (CH). The conclusion is conditional on the explicitly stated join-coherence and universal-range assumptions C1 and T5.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $P$ be a set of primitive type labels. A formal type $\\Gamma$ records a finitely supported amount function $\\nu_\\Gamma:P\\to\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}$, not identically zero. Primitive generators have their unit amount function. Positive scaling, separated product, and thermal-join constructors retain distinct formal tags and obey\n\n$$\\nu_{s\\Gamma}=s\\nu_\\Gamma,\\qquad \\nu_{\\Gamma\\otimes\\Gamma'}=\\nu_\\Gamma+\\nu_{\\Gamma'}\\qquad(s>0).$$\n\nSeparated and joined types are distinct constructors even when their amount functions agree. Permutation, reassociation, and scaled-splitting conventions preserve the amount function. Define the positive **bookkeeping weight** $w_\\Gamma:=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu_\\Gamma(p)$, a finite sum over support. Thus $w_{s\\Gamma}=s w_\\Gamma$ and every product or join has weight $w_\\Gamma+w_{\\Gamma'}$. The optional empty unit has zero amount function and weight zero; it is represented only by the empty compound, not as a simple chart. These are formal amounts, not masses in kilograms or an empirical conservation law. The functions and pointwise operations use [[def-function-space]]; their finite-support convention and finite sums are those of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] and [[lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined]].\n\nLet $I$ index the declared family of simple types $\\Gamma_i$. For each simple type $\\Gamma$ in that family, let $n_\\Gamma\\ge1$ and let $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_\\Gamma+1}$ be nonempty, open and convex, with coordinates $x=(u,v)$. The notation for a type and its chart is shared, but each state has a distinct raw tag $(\\Gamma,x)$. Scaled charts are distinct tagged copies $s\\Gamma=\\{sx:x\\in\\Gamma\\}$ with coordinate transport $x\\mapsto sx$; transported raw tags remain distinct from the original raw tags. Let $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$ be the unit point mass from [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]]. The represented state is\n\n$$\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x):=w_\\Gamma e_{(\\Gamma,x)},\\qquad m(\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x))=w_\\Gamma.$$\n\nWrite $\\mathsf{J}(x):=\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ when $x$ already carries its type tag. All state notation in this framework's compound comparisons, including a bare $x$, denotes $\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$; a raw unit point mass is always written $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$. A separated pair is addition of represented states, not a unit product tag. Nonnegative multiples are pointwise and zero terms are omitted. For an encoded compound $c$, its aggregate composition is the finite amount function\n\n$$\\nu(c):=\\sum_{(\\Gamma,x)\\in\\operatorname{supp}(c)}\\frac{c(\\Gamma,x)}{w_\\Gamma}\\nu_\\Gamma;\\qquad m(c)=\\sum_{p\\in P}\\nu(c)(p).$$\n\nFix the formal interpretation $\\Pi(c)$ as the finite separated preparation whose factor at each nonzero raw tag $(\\Gamma,x)$ is the amount $c(\\Gamma,x)/w_\\Gamma$ of that typed state, using the declared coordinate scaling transport. The accessibility relation on coefficient maps is the pullback of the declared relation on these preparations. Merging or splitting repeated copies of the same preparation is reversible; permutation and reassociation preserve the preparation. Thus $\\Pi(\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(sx))$ and $\\Pi(s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x))$ are the same declared scaled preparation and yield\n\n$$\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(sx)\\sim s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x),$$\n\nalthough their raw point masses have different tags and are not equal. This reversible transport commutes with finite composition, nested type constructors, and scaled splitting. It is part of the precise formal scaling interpretation, not a deduction about physical accessibility from coordinate multiplication. A physical use must adopt the stated preparation maps and accessibility assumptions; fixed-composition models compare only preparations with the same $\\nu$, while a declared channel model may additionally compare different compositions of equal $m$.\n\nA comparison $A\\prec B$ is defined only when $m(A)=m(B)$, as in the coefficient-map supplier. Write $A\\sim B$ when both directions hold, and $A\\prec\\prec B$ when only the forward direction holds. On any fixed simple type, dividing every represented state by the same positive constant $w_\\Gamma$ gives its unit-point-mass comparison. Positive scaling and its inverse preserve and reflect order; whenever the required CH and reference hypotheses hold, the unit-state entropy theorem applies after that normalization. If $S$ represents unit-point-mass comparisons, the entropy of a represented state is $w_\\Gamma S(x)$ and the entropy of a finite sum $\\sum_j t_j\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x_j)$ is $\\sum_j t_j w_\\Gamma S(x_j)$. No independent rescaling across different types is assumed without a calibrator.\n\nThe common relation satisfies A1–A6 on all equal-mass finite compounds: (A1) reflexivity; (A2) transitivity; (A3) if $A\\prec B$, then $A+C\\prec B+C$ for every compound $C$; (A4) if $A\\prec B$, then $tA\\prec tB$ for every $t>0$; (A5) $t\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ is equivalent to $s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)+(t-s)\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ for $t>0$ and $0<s<t$, with the reversible scaled-copy transport above; (A6) for equal-mass $A,B$, fixed positive equal-mass spectators $Z_0,Z_1$, and every sequence $\\epsilon_n>0$ decreasing to zero, $A+\\epsilon_n Z_0\\prec B+\\epsilon_n Z_1$ for all $n$ implies $A\\prec B$. These are hypotheses, not conclusions. No comparison hypothesis is included here.\n\nFor each $i$ and $x\\in\\Gamma_i$, define the forward sector $A_x:=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)\\prec\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(y)\\}$. The simple-domain assumptions are: (A7) for $x,y\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $0<t<1$, the encoded compound $t\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+(1-t)\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(y)$ compares to $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(tx+(1-t)y)$; both have bookkeeping mass $w_{\\Gamma_i}$, and convexity puts the recombined coordinate in the chart; (S1) each $x$ has a strict successor; (S2) $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane at $x$, expressible as $u-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0$ with a finite-valued slope map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$, and $P_i$ is locally Lipschitz; (S3) the boundary of $A_x$ relative to $\\Gamma_i$ is arcwise connected. A supporting hyperplane means that $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets the plane at $x$. The affine hull is the smallest affine subspace containing a set; relative interior is interior in that affine hull. Closure and boundary are taken in the indicated affine hull or in $\\Gamma_i$, as named; boundary is closure intersected with closure of the complement in that relative topology.\n\nThis definition imposes no physical interpretation, units, or empirical claim. A contact theorem that uses joins must separately assume that each join is a simple domain satisfying these same forward-sector hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix two simple types $i,j$ in the declared family. Give every primitive material its unit amount vector and use the aggregate composition of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]] for each scaled, separated or joined type $\\Gamma$; its sum is $w_\\Gamma>0$ unless the type is empty. Construct [F2]'s encoded state and interpretation maps using the physical tagged scaling maps: a raw coefficient $a>0$ at $(\\Gamma,x)$ denotes the preparation $(a/w_\\Gamma)x$. The map preserves aggregate composition and formal mass, and distinct raw tags remain distinct. The interpretations of $\\mathsf{J}_{s\\Gamma}(\\delta_s x)$ and $s\\mathsf{J}_\\Gamma(x)$ are the same declared scaled preparation by composition of scaling maps; reflexivity gives reversible transport. When factor coefficients combine or split, [A1]'s reversible-splitting axiom supplies reversibility, so the pullback inherits composition, positive scaling and stability from [A1]. Dividing fixed-type encoded maps by $w_\\Gamma$ and scaling back preserves and reflects comparisons, so the one-copy forward sectors and all geometry in [A2] are unchanged. Any common sign chosen in the mathematical orientation deduction is used only for proof coordinates $u'=\\varepsilon U$; the physical energy $U$ retains its fixed SI meaning, units and all independently adopted positive-$S_U$ or calibration qualifications. Since the same sign is applied to each component and join proof coordinate, summed-energy identities are preserved. Thus the actual preparation model supplies the exact formal conventions and all order/geometric hypotheses required by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [A3], the physical operation [[def-td-thermal-join]] supplies T1–T4 with the same summed-energy coordinates and reversible splitting used in [F1]. Its pair and join retain the same aggregate composition by [[def-td-thermal-join]], so their encoded weights both equal $w_i+w_j$; no mass-one join is compared to an unweighted mass-two pair.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A3",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "[A4] supplies the separately stated T5 quantifiers for every ordered simple-system pair, and [A5] supplies the separate C1 nested-join equivalence. Thus every premise of [F1], including its two stronger contact inputs, is met on the whole declared join-closed family.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A4",
            "A5",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1] to this pair $i,j$. Its first conclusion is states $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ with $(x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0)$; its second is comparison for every finite separated product made from scaled copies of these two types. This is precisely the stated pairwise conclusion.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement a finite family with no factors has only the empty product comparison, supplied by reflexivity and zero entropy. Actual pair calibration uses the separately assumed nonempty simple domains and admitted joins.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "For every declared finite family of simple-system domains closed under the thermal joins of [[def-td-thermal-join]], assume the accessibility axioms A1–A6, the simple-system assumptions A7 and S1–S3, the contact assumptions T1–T4, the universal thermal-equilibrium range T5, and equilibrium-join coherence C1, with the exact formulations in [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], [[post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms]], [[post-td-thermal-join-axioms]], [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]]. Then for any two simple types $i,j$, there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $i$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $j$ such that\n\n$$ (x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0). $$\n\nMoreover, for this fixed pair $i,j$, every finite separated product made only from positive scaled copies of $i$ and $j$ satisfies the comparison hypothesis: any two equal-formal-mass states $A,B$ of that product type satisfy $A\\prec B$ or $B\\prec A$. No comparison conclusion for products involving a third primitive type is contained in this pairwise result. These are conditional model deductions. In particular, the conclusion is not claimed from the printed Theorem 4.8 interface with T1–T4 alone; the local mathematical supplier explicitly requires the additional T5 and C1 branch.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "8b7f0538ef4cda71545e28623dcc2ffabc54654a73378d04a16d6c2a1f902085",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The simple-system hypotheses use positive scales; zero scale is not a contact state."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and step 3.1 permit i=j; the result is still conditional on the full contact assumptions."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The simple-domain hypothesis has n_i>=1; the one-coordinate thermometer is excluded."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interpolation parameter or endpoint limit appears in the calibrator conclusion."
        },
