    "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "pthm-td-contact-product-comparison",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every declared finite family of simple-system domains closed under the thermal joins of [[def-td-thermal-join]], assume the accessibility axioms A1–A6, the simple-system assumptions A7 and S1–S3, the contact assumptions T1–T4, the universal thermal-equilibrium range T5, and equilibrium-join coherence C1, with the exact formulations in [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], [[post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms]], [[post-td-thermal-join-axioms]], [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]]. Then for any two simple types $i,j$, there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $i$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $j$ such that\n\n$$ (x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0). $$\n\nMoreover, for this fixed pair $i,j$, every finite separated product made only from positive scaled copies of $i$ and $j$ satisfies the comparison hypothesis: any two equal-formal-mass states $A,B$ of that product type satisfy $A\\prec B$ or $B\\prec A$. No comparison conclusion for products involving a third primitive type is contained in this pairwise result. These are conditional model deductions. In particular, the conclusion is not claimed from the printed Theorem 4.8 interface with T1–T4 alone; the local mathematical supplier explicitly requires the additional T5 and C1 branch.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty set of primitive simple-system labels containing a distinguished meter label $m$. Assume every finite separated product is explicitly represented by its finitely many tagged elementary factors; positive scaling and finite product are the only identifications. Suppose the join-closed family satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator]], and fix a strict meter pair $z_0\\prec\\prec z_1$ in $\\Gamma_m$. Assume the Axiom of Choice. Then one can choose, for every $i\\in I$, an additive constant and positive scale for its same-type entropy so that\n\n$$S\\big((t_1i_1,x_1),\\ldots,(t_ni_n,x_n)\\big)=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$$\n\nis additive, extensive, and characterizes every same-composition comparison on every finite separated product. No conclusion is asserted for transitions that change composition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A4",
          "source": "post-td-simple-system-geometric-axioms",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For each simple-system chart $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$, $n_i\\ge1$, let $x=(u,v)$, with one energy coordinate $u$ and $n_i$ work coordinates $v$. The chart is a nonempty open convex domain. The simple-compressible physical case is the fixed-composition slice and embedding of [[def-td-thermal-join]], with $n_i=1$, $u=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $v=V/(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})$. A declared multi-work chart or joined chart instead supplies its own equilibrium endpoint domain and diagonal unit map: $U=u(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $W_a=v_a w_a$ for fixed positive work-coordinate units $w_a$. The numerical domain is identified with these physical endpoints by that supplied map, and accessibility is pulled back from the admitted endpoint relation. These identifications preserve the internal-energy meaning and fixed composition; extra work coordinates do not follow from the simple-compressible state definition. Adopt these assumptions for every state $x$ and for every thermal join treated as a simple system:\n\n**A7 (convex-combination accessibility).** For $0<t<1$, the separated scaled pair $(t x,(1-t)y)$ is accessible to the chart state $t x+(1-t)y$, whenever these states are in the declared domains.\n\nFor each $x$, define its forward sector $A_x=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:x\\prec y\\}$. Adopt:\n\n**S1 (strict successor).** Some $y\\in\\Gamma_i$ satisfies $x\\prec\\prec y$.\n\n**S2 (unique finite-slope support).** At $x=(u_x,v_x)$, $A_x$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane through $x$, and it can be written\n$$\nu-u_x+P_i(x)\\cdot(v-v_x)=0\n$$\nwith finite $P_i(x)\\in\\mathbb R^{n_i}$. The map $P_i:\\Gamma_i\\to\\mathbb R^{n_i}$ is locally Lipschitz: every point has a neighborhood on which one finite Lipschitz constant bounds $\\|P_i(x)-P_i(y)\\|/\\|x-y\\|$. A supporting hyperplane means $A_x$ lies in one of its closed half-spaces and meets it at $x$.\n\n**S3 (connected relative boundary).** The boundary $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$, taken in the topology relative to $\\Gamma_i$, is arcwise connected.\n\nThese are explicit adopted conditions. The slope $P_i$ is a geometric support parameter here; no equation identifying it with measured pressure is asserted. In particular, S1–S3 and A7 do not assert comparison hypothesis for products.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A5",
          "source": "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a family $I$ of primitive material labels. A tagged type $c\\in I$ retains its identity even if another type has the same coordinate domain. A positive scale $\\lambda>0$ is dimensionless and is represented by the separately tagged bulk copy $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and map $\\delta_\\lambda$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A finite separated compound is a finite tuple of tagged scaled systems, with permutation and reassociation identifications and an empty unit type. Its state is a tuple of constituent equilibrium states; its extensive coordinate tuple is the sum specified by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] whenever the pair is admitted there.\n\nThe formal composition vector of a compound records, for each primitive label $c$, the sum of its positive scale coefficients. Two compounds have the same composition exactly when these vectors agree. The distinct **formal mass** $m(A)$ is the sum of all scale coefficients in $A$, a dimensionless bookkeeping quantity; it is not physical mass in kilograms, mole amount, energy, or volume. Same-composition compounds have equal formal mass. For states $A,B$ of the same compound type, write $A\\prec B$ when the declared adiabatic-accessibility relation of [[def-td-adiabatic-accessibility]] admits that endpoint comparison. This definition does not infer accessibility from coordinate addition.\n\nAn equal-composition comparison is a comparison between states of types with the same formal composition vector. The later reaction-network definition may separately admit transitions between distinct composition types, but the mathematical supplier still requires equal formal mass at both endpoints. A physical application must state how its channel preserves the chosen formal mass and separately record its dimensioned conservation balances. The empty unit has one state and formal mass zero. No identity is imposed between a primitive type and a scaled tag, or between two different decompositions of a compound, beyond the explicitly declared reassociation, permutation, and bulk-composition maps.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix a finite set of primitive labels from $I$. For every pair in this set, [A2] supplies a strict calibrator and comparison hypothesis on all finite products formed from that pair; the relation also satisfies the six accessibility assumptions of the physical model. These are the pairwise contact inputs required by [A3], not a claim that the pair theorem alone compares products involving three or more primitive types. The factors have the explicit tagged finite representation in [A5].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "A5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $I$ is nonempty, choose a primitive type $m$ as the distinguished meter label. Applying [A2] to the ordered pair $(m,m)$ supplies a strict meter pair $z_0\\prec\\prec z_1$ in $\\Gamma_m$. For each $j\\ne m$, applying [A2] to the ordered pair $(j,m)$ supplies states $a_j,b_j\\in\\Gamma_j$ and $c_j,d_j\\in\\Gamma_m$ with $a_j\\prec\\prec b_j$, $c_j\\prec\\prec d_j$, and $(a_j,d_j)\\sim(b_j,c_j)$, exactly the calibrator orientation required by [A3]. Choice [A1] selects the reference states, same-type entropy representatives and calibrator data simultaneously across $I$, exactly as [A3] requires. Use the composition-preserving coefficient-map interpretation constructed in [A2]: each actual state is represented by its type-weighted point mass, and scalar amount $t$ has the encoded state value $t$ times that state entropy. In [A3], same-type unit-label representations are converted back to represented-state values by their positive type weights, so the calibration describes the original tagged preparations, not independently rescaled entropy units for joined types. Each compound contains only finitely many tagged factors. The empty unit has value zero as the empty entropy sum; every nonempty compound is a finite sum of tagged factor entropies. Applying [A3] gives a single function $\\widetilde S$ on the family of all such finite compounds, additive on concatenation, homogeneous under each positive scale, and order-equivalent to $\\prec$ for each equal-composition comparison. No infinite sum is introduced because every state has finite support.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "A1",
            "A5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $x,y$ lie in one simple chart and $0<t<1$. By [A4], the scaled pair $(t x,(1-t)y)$ compares to the recombined state $t x+(1-t)y$. The representation in step 2.1 turns that comparison into $$ t\\widetilde S(x)+(1-t)\\widetilde S(y)\\le \\widetilde S(t x+(1-t)y), $$ where additivity and extensivity evaluate the pair entropy. This is precisely concavity on the admitted convex chart; the endpoint weights $t=0,1$ give equality.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A4",
            "step 2.1",
            "A3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1 assigns the empty unit the empty sum 0 and treats nonempty compounds as finite sums."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Admitted nonempty factor scales are strictly positive. The separate empty unit is assigned the empty entropy sum zero in current proof2.1; no zero-scale simple chart is asserted.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $I$ be a nonempty, possibly infinite set of primitive system labels. Assume the declared family includes every finite separated product and every finite thermal join, and that every finite join-closed subfamily satisfies the hypotheses of [[pthm-td-contact-product-comparison]]. Also assume the explicit finite-factor representation of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]] and the Axiom of Choice. Then there is a single dimensionless function $\\widetilde S$ on all states of the declared finite-factor family that is additive under separation, extensive under every admitted positive scaling, and characterizes every equal-composition comparison:\n\n$$ A\\prec B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\widetilde S(A)\\le \\widetilde S(B). $$\n\nFor each simple chart, A7 also makes $\\widetilde S$ concave on every convex region where the stated recombination operation is admitted. The result does not set the entropy unit to $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, determine additive constants across different reaction compositions, or establish empirical validity of the contact assumptions.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "77c0fd94707d1e021e526602fbe8fcf68e70b30258a7be37e5a8e80ac370a4b7",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
