    "pthm-td-universal-mixing-entropy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-entropy-under-product-comparison",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty, possibly infinite set of primitive system labels. Assume the declared family includes every finite separated product and every finite thermal join, and that every finite join-closed subfamily satisfies the hypotheses of [[pthm-td-contact-product-comparison]]. Also assume the explicit finite-factor representation of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]] and the Axiom of Choice. Then there is a single dimensionless function $\\widetilde S$ on all states of the declared finite-factor family that is additive under separation, extensive under every admitted positive scaling, and characterizes every equal-composition comparison:\n\n$$ A\\prec B\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\widetilde S(A)\\le \\widetilde S(B). $$\n\nFor each simple chart, A7 also makes $\\widetilde S$ concave on every convex region where the stated recombination operation is admitted. The result does not set the entropy unit to $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, determine additive constants across different reaction compositions, or establish empirical validity of the contact assumptions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-cross-type-reaction-network",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A cross-type mixing and reaction network consists of a declared set $\\mathcal G$ of tagged composition types and a state set $X_\\Gamma$ for each type. Each state set comes with a fixed formal composition $\\nu_\\Gamma$, and its states satisfy $\\nu(x)=\\nu_\\Gamma$ as in [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. These mathematical coefficients and their positive type weights are distinct from dimensioned material amounts. For physical channels the model additionally supplies endpoint quantity maps $U_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ and $n_\\Gamma:X_\\Gamma\\to\\mathbb R^r\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ in a declared finite species list, together with any separately tracked mechanical or external-field endpoint quantities. No identification of two distinct scaled tags is made.\n\nFor each channel $\\alpha$ from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$, the model supplies sets $Z_\\alpha$ of configurations, $\\mathcal K_\\alpha$ of protocol records, $\\mathcal B_\\alpha$ of outer-boundary records, and $\\mathcal R_\\alpha$ of transfer records, and equilibrium subsets and endpoint maps $\\xi^-_\\alpha:Z^-_\\alpha\\to X_\\Gamma$, $\\xi^+_\\alpha:Z^+_\\alpha\\to X_{\\Gamma'}$. A typed record is\n$$P=(I,x^-,x^+,z,\\kappa,b,r),$$\nwhere $I=[t_0,t_1]$, $t_0<t_1$, is a finite time interval in seconds, $x^-\\in X_\\Gamma$, $x^+\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$, $z:I\\to Z_\\alpha$, $z(t_0)\\in Z^-_\\alpha$, $z(t_1)\\in Z^+_\\alpha$, $\\xi^-_\\alpha(z(t_0))=x^-$, $\\xi^+_\\alpha(z(t_1))=x^+$, $\\kappa\\in\\mathcal K_\\alpha$, $b\\in\\mathcal B_\\alpha$, and $r\\in\\mathcal R_\\alpha$. Transfer maps $Q_\\alpha,W_{\\mathrm{on},\\alpha}:\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ and $N_\\alpha:\\mathcal R_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R^r\\,\\mathrm{mol}$ record heat, work and material transfer with declared signs; any tracked $\\Delta K_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ and $\\Delta U_{\\mathrm{ext}}$ are also joule-valued record maps. The channel specifies a balance predicate on this typed record set, or equivalently a balance map into a declared product of quantity spaces whose required value is zero, and a model-admitted subset satisfying that predicate. The predicate states the actual energy and material constraints of that channel; no balance law, physical admission or endpoint realizability follows from the tuple alone. For a fixed-constitution core closed channel, its endpoint embeddings, fields and admitted subset are exactly those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], with no material transfer and its stated kinetic/external restrictions. Other channels require their own adopted constraints. Define $(\\Gamma,x)\\mathcal R(\\Gamma',y)$ exactly when an admitted channel record has those endpoints. No channel is inferred from separated composition.\n\nFor any permitted equal-formal-mass compound states $A,B$ of the network, define $A\\prec B$ exactly when at least one admitted channel record has initial state $A$ and final state $B$; for named single types this is the relation $\\mathcal R$ above. This is the exact relation supplied to [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], not a separate abstract order that enlarges or restricts the channel edges. Its extension to finite tagged products and common spectators is governed by the adopted A1–A6 conditions below. Thus every edge in a decorated chain is precisely an admitted channel transition. This identification is a declared model condition.\n\nThe network is **finite-factor closed** when its types admit the declared finite composition and positive scaling operations and each allowed channel respects the model’s stated conservation constraints. In addition, each channel must preserve the dimensionless formal mass $m$ of [[def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison]]; this is the equal-coefficient-mass convention required by the mathematical chain-cost supplier, not a statement that formal mass is kilograms or moles. The common relation on all equal-formal-mass compounds satisfies A1–A6 of [[post-td-accessibility-a1-a6]], including consistency with spectators and stability. A decorated finite chain is exactly a finite sequence of such allowed transitions with a common catalyst and the real baseline-entropy edge costs in [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]].\n\nFor $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$, let $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty,+\\infty\\}$ be the infimum of those finite decorated-chain costs, with $+\\infty$ if no witness exists and $-\\infty$ if costs are unbounded below. The network has **no sinks** when, for every ordered type pair,\n\n$$F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)<+\\infty.$$\n\nNo-sinks is an explicit property required of a network used in the global-offset theorem; it is not proved by this definition, and it is not asserted for every physical mixing or reaction. The definition permits the channel set to be empty; then the condition must still be checked rather than inferred.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the calibrated entropy family of [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]], the cross-type accessibility axioms A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and the decorated cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. For every pair of types $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$ and states $x\\in X_\\Gamma$, $y\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$,\n\n$$x\\prec y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le \\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y).$$\n\nThe infimum defining $F$ need not be attained. If $F=+\\infty$, the right side is false; if $F=-\\infty$, the right side is true.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-splitting-cost-quotient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $F$ be the decorated cost of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], and assume the **no-sinks condition**\n\n$$F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)<+\\infty$$\n\nfor every type pair. Let $V_F$ be the real difference space of [[def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space]]. Then there is an additive extensive offset $B:\\mathcal G\\to\\mathbb R$ such that\n\n$$-F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)\\le B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma').$$\n\nIf every admitted cross-type transition $x\\prec y$ from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ satisfies the calibrated cost inequality of [[thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference]], then $\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)$ is nondecreasing across every such transition. Only this weak inequality is asserted; no-gap equalities and strict extensions are not concluded.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix an admitted transition $(\\Gamma,x)\\mathcal R(\\Gamma',y)$. [F1] supplies finite baseline state values on both endpoint types, and [F2] identifies the decorated-chain cost convention used for this pair. Use the composition-preserving type-weighted representation from [F1]'s construction: interpret each encoded coefficient by its amount divided by the positive type weight, keeping the raw scaled and joined tags distinct. The network compares equal formal masses, and this interpretation preserves its admitted endpoint relation and each finite baseline entropy sum; hence each physical finite-chain witness and its coefficient-map image have the same real edge costs. Reversible regrouping of factors uses the adopted reversible-splitting axiom in [F2], so it changes neither the represented endpoints nor their baseline values. The cost $F$ used by [F3] is therefore this exact witness infimum, not one inferred from coordinate equality. Applying [F3] to this exact transition gives $$ \\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)\\ge F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma'). $$ If the edge exists, the one-edge chain makes $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty$, so this inequality has an ordinary real right side under the stated no-sinks hypothesis.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The network’s no-sinks condition and [A1] match the hypotheses of [F4]; its cost is the same $F$ as in step 1.1 by [F2] and [F3]. Thus [F4] supplies one offset function $B$, common to all types, with additivity, positive-scale extensivity and $B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ for every pair.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Subtract the bound in step 2.1 from that in step 1.1: $$ [\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)+B(\\Gamma')]-[\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)]\\ge0. $$ By the definition of $S$ in the Statement, this is $S_{\\Gamma'}(y)\\ge S_\\Gamma(x)$. The same $B$ works for every admitted transition, proving the conditional weak entropy principle.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
