    "cex-td-degenerate-thermometer-comparison": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-scaled-products-and-comparison",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a family $I$ of primitive material labels. A tagged type $c\\in I$ retains its identity even if another type has the same coordinate domain. A positive scale $\\lambda>0$ is dimensionless and is represented by the separately tagged bulk copy $c^{(\\lambda)}$ and map $\\delta_\\lambda$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. A finite separated compound is a finite tuple of tagged scaled systems, with permutation and reassociation identifications and an empty unit type. Its state is a tuple of constituent equilibrium states; its extensive coordinate tuple is the sum specified by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] whenever the pair is admitted there.\n\nThe formal composition vector of a compound records, for each primitive label $c$, the sum of its positive scale coefficients. Two compounds have the same composition exactly when these vectors agree. The distinct **formal mass** $m(A)$ is the sum of all scale coefficients in $A$, a dimensionless bookkeeping quantity; it is not physical mass in kilograms, mole amount, energy, or volume. Same-composition compounds have equal formal mass. For states $A,B$ of the same compound type, write $A\\prec B$ when the declared adiabatic-accessibility relation of [[def-td-adiabatic-accessibility]] admits that endpoint comparison. This definition does not infer accessibility from coordinate addition.\n\nAn equal-composition comparison is a comparison between states of types with the same formal composition vector. The later reaction-network definition may separately admit transitions between distinct composition types, but the mathematical supplier still requires equal formal mass at both endpoints. A physical application must state how its channel preserves the chosen formal mass and separately record its dimensioned conservation balances. The empty unit has one state and formal mass zero. No identity is imposed between a primitive type and a scaled tag, or between two different decompositions of a compound, beyond the explicitly declared reassociation, permutation, and bulk-composition maps.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-thermal-join-axioms",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Use thermal joins and $\\sim_T$ exactly as defined in [[def-td-thermal-join]]. For every ordered pair of simple systems and every admitted finite join, adopt:\n\n**T1 (joining).** $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$.\n\n**T2 (reversible splitting).** Every join state has at least one split into component states at its stated total energy and work coordinates that is reversible to the join state. Also, for every state $x$ and every $0<t<1$, the equal-state scaled split $(t x,(1-t)x)$ is reversible to $x$.\n\n**T3 (thermal-equilibrium transitivity).** If $x\\sim_T y$ and $y\\sim_T z$ in the model’s declared contact family, then $x\\sim_T z$. This requires the model to provide the relevant joined types and identifications.\n\n**T4 (transversality).** For each simple-system state $x$, there are $x_0,x_1$ in that system with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$.\n\nThe axioms apply to admitted types and their joins only. T4 is an assumption about the contact relation; it is not a consequence of an equation of state or of a thermometer reading. The stronger range condition T5 is stated separately in [[post-td-universal-thermal-range]], and join coherence C1 is separately stated in [[post-td-equilibrium-join-coherence]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Start at $(1,3)$. Then $\\bar U=2$, every point of $A$ has second coordinate at least $3$, and every point of $B$ has second coordinate at least $2$. A finite convex combination of points whose second coordinates are at least $2$ still has second coordinate at least $2$, because it is the weighted sum of those coordinates with nonnegative weights summing to one. Hence every point in $\\operatorname{conv}(A\\cup B)$ has second coordinate at least $2$, so $(3,1)$ is not in the forward sector and $(1,3)\\not\\prec(3,1)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-2",
          "claim": "In the one-system space, strict accessibility is exactly strict increase of $U$, while thermal equilibrium is equality of normalized energies. If $U_0\\prec\\prec U\\prec\\prec U_1$, then $U_0<U<U_1$, so $U_0\\ne U_1$ and the two cannot be thermally equivalent. Thus T4 fails.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "Start instead at $(3,1)$. Again $\\bar U=2$; every point of its $A$ has first coordinate at least $3$, and every point of its $B$ has first coordinate at least $2$. Convex combinations therefore have first coordinate at least $2$, so $(1,3)$ is not accessible from $(3,1)$. The two product states are incomparable, and the comparison hypothesis fails.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The single-system function $S(U)=\\log U$ is strictly increasing and represents $U\\le U'$. If its additive sum represented product accessibility, the states $(1,3)$ and $(3,1)$ would have equal summed entropy $\\log 3$, forcing accessibility both ways. Step 1.1 and step 2.1 show neither direction holds. Therefore this single-system entropy does not represent the product relation. Since the example fails T4 by step 1.2, it does not satisfy the hypotheses of a product-comparison result that includes T4.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The counterexample uses two positive-energy thermometer copies, not the empty unit."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "All thermometer energies and scale coefficients in the witness are positive."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.2 checks the one-system T4 condition separately from the two-copy product failure."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The state space has one energy coordinate; step 1.2 proves T4 fails."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interpolation endpoint is part of this counterexample."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and steps 1.1–1.2 use two positive unit copies and no choice principle."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 1.2 prove neither product comparison direction holds."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 1.2 prove neither product comparison direction holds."
        }
      ],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
    "def-td-cross-type-reaction-network": {
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Definition explicitly permits an empty channel set and requires its no-sinks condition to be checked; a pair with no decorated-chain witness has cost +infinity."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The Definition does not infer accessibility or zero cost from zero formal mass; the relation requires an admitted channel record."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No separate singleton-network or one-step-chain theorem is asserted by this Definition."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Definition recovers the fixed-composition process record when the endpoint composition types agree and permits an empty channel set."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Definition requires equilibrium initial and final states and full channel protocol, outer-boundary, energy and auxiliary data; edges are exactly admitted records with those endpoints."
        },
